--TEST--
Core\Password::verify has two refusals and the line between them is which argument was wrong — a password it does not accept is `false`, including an empty one, while a stored value that is not a hash throws — spec § 16, ADR 0063
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `verify`'s whole failure surface, asserted as a shape rather than one case
// at a time: every way the *password* can be wrong is the same `false`, and
// every way the *stored hash* can be wrong is a throw. PHP has one answer for
// both — `password_verify` returns `false` for a malformed hash too — which is
// why a storage layer handing back the wrong column looks exactly like every
// user typing the wrong password at once. `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/password.rs`
// is the home of that divergence and
// `password-refuses-a-stored-value-that-is-not-a-hash-it-wrote.nvst` pins the
// messages; what this case adds is that the two sides are counted together, so
// a member that moved one input from one side to the other fails here while
// still reading correctly on its own line.
secret string $password = "correct horse battery staple";
string $stored = Core\Password::hash($password);
string $anothersHash = Core\Password::hash("hunter2");

int $refused = 0;
int $threw = 0;
int $accepted = 0;

// Four ways the password is wrong. The empty one is named because it is the
// value an unfilled form field arrives as, and because a member that treated
// it as "nothing to check" would have to answer something other than `false`.
secret string $wrong = "hunter2";
secret string $empty = "";
secret string $oneByteLonger = "correct horse battery staples";

if (Core\Password::verify($wrong, $stored)) {
    $accepted = $accepted + 1;
} else {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
if (Core\Password::verify($empty, $stored)) {
    $accepted = $accepted + 1;
} else {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
if (Core\Password::verify($oneByteLonger, $stored)) {
    $accepted = $accepted + 1;
} else {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
// The right password against a hash of a different one: right secret, wrong
// row, and still an answer rather than a failure.
if (Core\Password::verify($password, $anothersHash)) {
    $accepted = $accepted + 1;
} else {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}

// Three ways the stored value is wrong. Each is a storage bug rather than a
// login attempt, so none of them is allowed to reach the `false` above — and
// none is a fatal either, which is what the `catch` proves: the request is
// still running afterwards to print the count.
try {
    bool $notAHash = Core\Password::verify($password, "not a stored hash");
    $accepted = $accepted + 1;
} catch (LogicError $garbage) {
    $threw = $threw + 1;
}
try {
    bool $fromAnEmptyColumn = Core\Password::verify($password, "");
    $accepted = $accepted + 1;
} catch (LogicError $nothingStored) {
    $threw = $threw + 1;
}
try {
    bool $foreign = Core\Password::verify($password, '$pbkdf2$v=19$m=19456,t=2,p=1$jJ+hokoSJRsAzYsgfwhV6g$BGyWXoH11l0/GF4ezLqvtQgPY0T/4fv4DukErq9R0cI');
    $accepted = $accepted + 1;
} catch (LogicError $notOurs) {
    $threw = $threw + 1;
}

echo "refused=", $refused, " threw=", $threw, " accepted=", $accepted, "\n";

// And the member has not simply become one that refuses everything.
if (Core\Password::verify($password, $stored)) {
    echo "and the right password against its own hash is accepted\n";
} else {
    echo "refused the right password\n";
}
--EXPECT--
refused=4 threw=3 accepted=0
and the right password against its own hash is accepted
